artment i think it's  around 30 percent women and math major  and then when you go to grad school  there are maybe 18 20  women and when you go to faculty well  there are four of us  is there one small thing that educators  could do to try to make this situation  better  yeah i think being aware  that they themselves have implicit  biases would be already a big step  and i do have implicit biases myself i i  know that  but i'm aware of it so i'm trying to go  in the opposite direction i will  actually be tempted to go without  thinking about it so i think being aware  of the issue being aware that when you  ask a question new math class you might  be a woman teacher you look at the boys  the girls which is something that  happens very often being aware of that  that's already a big step so next time  you try to do  you know 50 50. i mean if all the  problems are about baseball players who  and soccers and well now the girl do  soccer they are even better than men so  that's great but you know  there were problems that were written 15  years ago and they were all for boys  because those those are the  people that will take those classes so  being aware of this  and what difference could make  if you just you phrase the problem you  know in a different way i think it's  very important  at the end of the day deliola hopes that  students will feel invested in the  questions they're answering in calculus  one  we put together a lot of problems that  come from concepts that we see in real  life that really helps i think also in  accepting  the difficulties that you face when you  actually have to solve a problem if it's  connected with something which is more  real you  are more willing to work through that  so for example one problem that i like  which actually has to do with solving  some linear equations is a question on  how many bagels i'm supposed to  buy and versus how many croissan and how  many if the classes want a certain you  know likes more sweets or salty or  things like that so it's a it's a simple  problem but then when you have to  translate into math and find a solution  you have to use relatively sophisticated  tools to do that if there's more than  one variable so right with an important  outcome too yeah right  i usually bring that stuff in class  afterwards crosstalk and bagels and  donuts  and then i try to say how would you  attack this how would you do this or  maybe some questions from biology or  from question from chemistry or  something that has at least the first  step which one can understand using  calculus to give the idea that this is  not like some abstract nonsense that we  force you to take at mit but it's  something that's very important to  like i said from the very beginning it's  a language that you need to acquire in  order to  attack any basic questions that might  come up in science in general which has  not already been answered right which is  something that they  love to be in the position of doing in  your new life right to answer a new  question  [Music]  if you're interested in teaching with g  leola's calculus materials head over to  mit's open learning library site where  you'll find her single variable calculus  moocs  thank you so much for listening until  next time signing off from cambridge  massachusetts i'm your host sarah hansen  from mit open courseware  mit chalk radio's producers include  myself brett pache and dave lashansky  our script writers are nidhishastri and  aubry callaway  show notes for this episode were written  by peter chipman  we're funded by mit open learning and  supporters like you  you

